{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed g, m_med, and Lambda_ff, dsigma_dq2 falls as a single monotone product of a screened propagator factor and one fixed dipole-like form-factor shape, so the log-slope versus q**2 changes in only one prescribed way.",
      "child_relation": "Keep the same variables but replace the current fixed form-factor profile with another admissible monotone elastic profile, changing how the suppression bends with q**2 once the transfer approaches the composite-resolution scale.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "q",
        "Lambda_ff"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "For q**2 comparable to Lambda_ff**2, the cross section develops a different intermediate-to-high-q**2 curvature than the parent; after matching the low-q**2 normalization and one slope scale, a single parent parameter set cannot simultaneously reproduce the altered tail steepening pattern across the same q range."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "prop_screen",
        "expression": "q**2/m_med**2",
        "role": "screening importance in the propagator denominator"
      },
      {
        "name": "resolution_ratio",
        "expression": "q**2/Lambda_ff**2",
        "role": "degree to which momentum transfer resolves composite structure"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when q**2 is scanned through the resolved-structure regime q**2 ~ Lambda_ff**2 to several times Lambda_ff**2, while remaining in the sampled nonzero-transfer region and with fixed g and m_med.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism already changes the bend of dsigma_dq2 as q**2 moves from well below to around Lambda_ff**2; in holdout at somewhat larger q**2 the same modified profile produces a systematically different tail curvature rather than a mere rescaling.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit where the alternative elastic profile is specialized back to the current dipole-like form-factor assumption.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The model assumes one specific composite elastic form-factor shape, so all structure-induced suppression is forced into a single dipole-like q**2 dependence.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The external particles' resolved internal structure is represented by the fixed factor (1 + q**2/Lambda_ff**2)**(-2) in the amplitude-squared, rather than by another physically admissible monotone elastic profile.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "A different but still elastic and nonresonant spatial charge/current distribution of the composite scatterer changes the transfer-space form factor shape, modifying the q**2-dependent curvature of the differential cross section once the probe resolves internal extent.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "q",
      "Lambda_ff"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: with g, m_med, and Lambda_ff fixed, dsigma_dq2 decreases monotonically with q through the propagator and one prescribed form-factor profile. The scientific gap is not a missing channel but an idealized assumption that the composite profile is uniquely dipole-like. A realistic next refinement is to relax that shape assumption while keeping the same static inputs. The changed target mapping is in the scored observable itself, not only in an auxiliary quantity: varying q at fixed g, m_med, and Lambda_ff produces a different curvature in dsigma_dq2 once q**2 reaches the resolution scale set by Lambda_ff. Concretely, in the admissible regime q**2 << Lambda_ff**2 both parent and child can agree closely because the form factor tends to unity, but for q**2 ~ Lambda_ff**2 and above the child shows a different rate of tail suppression. A single refitted parent parameter set cannot generally absorb this across those conditions because changing Lambda_ff mostly shifts the turnover location, whereas the refinement changes the shape of the turnover and high-q**2 bend itself. This is scientifically observable only when the sampled q range reaches the resolved-structure regime; if all data satisfy q**2 << Lambda_ff**2, the distinction becomes weak and the refinement would be hard to identify."
  },
